# A value-position `try` join one branch leaves `null` (#86 §3). The temp carries the join's type, and a branch whose
# result is a bare `null` literal assigns NOTHING — the temp keeps its default — so a bare `int32` temp would answer
# that branch with `0` and not with a null. Widened to `Nullable<int32>`, the default IS `HasValue=false`.
#
# No Kotlin source in the corpus reaches this shape today: the frontend keeps the `?` on the join type wherever it
# can, and the whole stdlib lowers byte-identically with the widening in place. The shape is nonetheless real — a
# substituted generic or a spliced inline body resolves the join to a bare `Int` with the `?` on the enclosing
# return — so this document is the witness, exactly as the reject-* documents are for the refusals.
+"name":"dotkt$tryval0","type":{"t":"nullable","of":{"t":"fqn","name":"System.Int32"}}
-"name":"dotkt$tryval0","type":{"t":"fqn","name":"System.Int32"}

# The two DISCRIMINATIONS, without which the assertion above would also pass a pass that widened every join. A join
# whose branches all yield values keeps its bare value type; a REFERENCE join keeps its bare reference type, because
# a reference slot holds a null already and `Nullable<string>` is not a CLR type at all.
+"name":"dotkt$tryval1","type":{"t":"fqn","name":"System.Int32"}
+"name":"dotkt$tryval2","type":{"t":"fqn","name":"System.String"}
